\n\nSo, the probability distribution becomes\n\n![](images/9a/9a6f0b77892fb76598596992be35903b302ce14191b448a1adcce1f518b3612a.svg)\n\nFinally, we calculate ![](images/81/81b8764208b2f6a31a51bf2f27f709db42357e4c09c83e33432dd205b2c172aa.svg) as follows:\n\n![](images/e9/e9e12db3d55238bb5b33f68a325ede84caa1c37d4cd97f3e4eee3c75975d8796.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161733 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c91d137-d318-5d98-9dc4-0b701a767b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Percentage Points Table To Find a Chi-Square Probability"}] :question/id #uuid "1c328fe8-ba46-51f0-acb3-db3781a9d859" :question/math-academy-id "e-16429" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\geq x) = p,$ where $X\\sim \\chi^2(k).$ Given that the random variable $X \\sim \\chi^2(7),$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X \\geq x) = 0.99.$\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $\\,0.412\\,$ | $\\,0.554\\,$ | $\\,0.831\\,$ | $\\,1.145\\,$ | $\\,1.610\\,$ |\n| $6$ | $\\,0.676\\,$ | $\\,0.872\\,$ | $\\,1.237\\,$ | $\\,1.635\\,$ | $\\,2.204\\,$ |\n| $7$ | $\\,0.989\\,$ | $\\,1.239\\,$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.167\\,$ | $\\,2.833\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Since $X \\sim \\chi^2(7),$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=7.$\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,0.989\\,$ | $\\,1.239\\,$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.167\\,$ | $\\,2.833\\,$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 1.239) = 0.990.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=1.239.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161734 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec19c717-37bc-53c3-b09e-21a2fd2b77fa" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Chi-Square Probability Using the Complement"}] :question/id #uuid "42104104-5483-5c90-be3f-dbe7b10cbff3" :question/math-academy-id "e-16430" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\geq x) = p,$ where $X\\sim \\chi^2(k).$ Given that $X \\sim \\chi^2(7),$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X \\leq x) = 0.005.$\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $\\,0.412\\,$ | $\\,0.554\\,$ | $\\,0.831\\,$ | $\\,1.145\\,$ | $\\,1.610\\,$ |\n| $6$ | $\\,0.676\\,$ | $\\,0.872\\,$ | $\\,1.237\\,$ | $\\,1.635\\,$ | $\\,2.204\\,$ |\n| $7$ | $\\,0.989\\,$ | $\\,1.239\\,$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.167\\,$ | $\\,2.833\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "The table shows probabilities in the form $P(X > x).$ So first, we express the desired probability in this form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>x) & =1-P(X\\le x) \\\\  & =1-0.005 \\\\  & =0.995\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X \\sim \\chi^2(7),$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=7.$\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,0.989\\,$ | $\\,1.239\\,$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.167\\,$ | $\\,2.833\\,$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X > 0.989) = 0.995.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=0.989.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161735 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a75e6c3-16e3-50ab-bd36-a2c83ba9288a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability Involving Sums of Squared Standard Normal Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "ab5d8857-fdb8-5f4f-85cc-ba9d9e480081" :question/math-academy-id "e-16432" :question/problem "Let the random variable $Y$ be defined as\n\n$$\nY = Z_1^2+Z_2^2+\\cdots + Z_{10}^2\n$$\n\nwhere $Z_i\\sim N(0,1)$ for $1\\leq i\\leq 10.$ Find the value $y$ that satisfies $P(Y\\leq y) = 0.99.$\n\n*Hint: The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\geq x) = p,$ where $X\\sim \\chi^2(k).$*\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $8$ | $\\,20.090\\,$ | $\\,15.507\\,$ | $\\,13.362\\,$ | $\\,12.027\\,$ | $\\,11.030\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,21.666\\,$ | $\\,16.919\\,$ | $\\,14.684\\,$ | $\\,13.288\\,$ | $\\,12.242\\,$ |\n| $10$ | $\\,23.209\\,$ | $\\,18.307\\,$ | $\\,15.987\\,$ | $\\,14.534\\,$ | $\\,13.442\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "If $Z_i\\sim N(0,1)$ for $1\\leq i \\leq k$ are independent normal random variables, then the sum\n\n$$\nZ_1^2+Z_2^2+\\cdots + Z_{k}^2\n$$\n\nfollows a $\\chi^2(k)$ distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\nY = Z_1^2+Z_2^2+\\cdots + Z_{10}^2 \\sim \\chi^2(10).\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the given probability in the form $P(Y\\geq y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le y) & =1-P(Y\\ge y) \\\\ 0.99 & =1-P(Y\\ge y) \\\\ P(Y\\ge y) & =0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we wish to find the value $y$ such that $P(Y\\geq y) = 0.01$ This can be found from the given table:\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $8$ | $\\,20.090\\,$ | $\\,15.507\\,$ | $\\,13.362\\,$ | $\\,12.027\\,$ | $\\,11.030\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,21.666\\,$ | $\\,16.919\\,$ | $\\,14.684\\,$ | $\\,13.288\\,$ | $\\,12.242\\,$ |\n| $10$ | $\\,\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}23.209}\\,$ | $\\,18.307\\,$ | $\\,15.987\\,$ | $\\,14.534\\,$ | $\\,13.442\\,$ |\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\geq 23.209) = 0.01\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\nP(Y\\leq 23.209) = 0.99.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value $y$ that satisfies $P(Y\\leq y) = 0.99$ is $y=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}23.209}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c91d137-d318-5d98-9dc4-0b701a767b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Percentage Points Table To Find a Chi-Square Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e6f5ea9-b1e5-53dc-94ee-55cb87ceb89e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158631} {:answer/id #uuid "da02eab8-ed7f-5dab-b1ee-57eb6eabc16a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.297" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158632} {:answer/id #uuid "1e7af3fd-4452-5e37-a8ff-601eec6ff8de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.554" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158633} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0e55a4-179a-5313-9fb1-c335c2b9ce63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.412" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158634} {:answer/id #uuid "7127ac09-9a99-5bf7-96ac-2a04827eacc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.610" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e7af3fd-4452-5e37-a8ff-601eec6ff8de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.554" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158633} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd00ac13-7639-5da6-828a-279c701cd404" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3878995-f0e7-5a70-8415-579b84758a63" :question/math-academy-id "q-105788" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X∼{χ}^{2}(k).$  Given that the random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(5),$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X\\ge x)=0.99.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $\\,0.072\\,$ | $\\,0.115\\,$ | $\\,0.216\\,$ | $\\,0.352\\,$ | $\\,0.584\\,$ |\n| $4$ | $\\,0.207\\,$ | $\\,0.297\\,$ | $\\,0.484\\,$ | $\\,0.711\\,$ | $\\,1.064\\,$ |\n| $5$ | $\\,0.412\\,$ | $\\,0.554\\,$ | $\\,0.831\\,$ | $\\,1.145\\,$ | $\\,1.610\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Since $X∼{χ}^{2}(5),$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=5.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $\\,0.412\\,$ | $\\,0.554\\,$ | $\\,0.831\\,$ | $\\,1.145\\,$ | $\\,1.610\\,$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 0.554)=0.99.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=0.554.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f80bc6ce-5b86-5883-a2bb-dbdc1e5503de" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability for a Chi-Square Random Variable Using the PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0823d426-5b2c-5ac6-b64a-d3179098eb29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8528" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434065} {:answer/id #uuid "2454fa59-6173-5a32-9c1b-31dc5e951511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3253" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434067} {:answer/id #uuid "5dcf242e-be31-5257-85a8-128cc12f3f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.3288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434075} {:answer/id #uuid "71f6e868-dd1f-5adf-83d1-cf86121aa303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8528" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434077} {:answer/id #uuid "cf6d6f91-79a7-500f-bfdc-0476ab995d9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3528" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf6d6f91-79a7-500f-bfdc-0476ab995d9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3528" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434085} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d46e94c-f4ab-56b9-8e85-c4389bca8a23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8899331b-91a4-5c31-88b7-72adb8fd73b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-108162" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(8),$ calculate $P(X<6).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x≈33.86574\n$$\n\n*The probability density function ${χ}^{2}(k)$ is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2})}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A chi-square random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(k)$ with $k$ degrees of freedom has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2})}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(8).$ So, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16Γ(4)}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecalling that $Γ(n)=(n-1)!$ for any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(4)=3!=6.\n$$\n\nSo, the probability distribution becomes\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $P(X<6)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<6) & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈\\frac{1}{96}\\cdot 33.86574 \\\\  & ≈0.3528\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f80bc6ce-5b86-5883-a2bb-dbdc1e5503de" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability for a Chi-Square Random Variable Using the PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2efa2847-8c8c-50f1-b144-1c847e19fa35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{x}^{6}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434069} {:answer/id #uuid "591bfd80-a947-5044-b7df-6ae75531398e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{x}^{4}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434073} {:answer/id #uuid "5c72f2e6-f2e4-5cc9-b22d-6cda68c3cf7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434074} {:answer/id #uuid "96eba1c0-859c-5458-a39c-079d9f2b36ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434082} {:answer/id #uuid "dce09a64-208d-5c8a-9fbb-34460d664adf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96eba1c0-859c-5458-a39c-079d9f2b36ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434082} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ee5e6c9-fa4a-58fc-ae2e-8a2fef0c2570" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cdd1fbde-a478-5539-a14c-9c15409e9daa" :question/math-academy-id "q-108186" :question/problem "Given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(8),$ what is $P(X<6)$ expressed as an integral?\n\n*Hint: The probability density function ${χ}^{2}(k)$ is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2})}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A chi-square random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(k)$ with $k$ degrees of freedom has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2})}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(8).$ So, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16Γ(4)}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecalling that $Γ(n)=(n-1)!$ for any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(4)=3!=6.\n$$\n\nSo, the probability distribution becomes\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $P(X<6)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<6) & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434094 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c91d137-d318-5d98-9dc4-0b701a767b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Percentage Points Table To Find a Chi-Square Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37aa76aa-7b11-559a-bc29-93953852d106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.676" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434071} {:answer/id #uuid "4a171e19-08b7-5dfb-88ec-745eba58dd09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.204" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434072} {:answer/id #uuid "78259eea-9d16-59de-adab-2a8608c1fee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.733" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434078} {:answer/id #uuid "9060b685-b5b9-5d63-9a14-913d1e947150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.490" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434080} {:answer/id #uuid "92d4a73f-849b-57cb-8c77-3eb3d9c58a2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.646" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78259eea-9d16-59de-adab-2a8608c1fee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.733" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434078} :answer-field/id #uuid "3542c59a-a8e5-5ff3-a238-b0c908d6f40c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4082c7b9-656c-5af0-a7d3-4e13cbac6782" :question/math-academy-id "q-108266" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X∼{χ}^{2}(k).$  Given that the random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(8),$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X>x)=0.950.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $6$ | $\\,0.676\\,$ | $\\,0.872\\,$ | $\\,1.237\\,$ | $\\,1.635\\,$ | $\\,2.204\\,$ |\n| $7$ | $\\,0.989\\,$ | $\\,1.239\\,$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.167\\,$ | $\\,2.833\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.344\\,$ | $\\,1.646\\,$ | $\\,2.180\\,$ | $\\,2.733\\,$ | $\\,3.490\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Since $X∼{χ}^{2}(8),$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=8.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.344\\,$ | $\\,1.646\\,$ | $\\,2.180\\,$ | $\\,2.733\\,$ | $\\,3.490\\,$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X>2.733)=0.950.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=2.733.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec19c717-37bc-53c3-b09e-21a2fd2b77fa" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Chi-Square Probability Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fb991ce-4b7c-5de9-ab03-280608b86db1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.484" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434066} {:answer/id #uuid "29c35df3-8e92-5fc7-a88c-ae44748bd3b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.344" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434068} {:answer/id #uuid "36a9fc04-92a0-545c-a9b1-c789f48da005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.084" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434070} {:answer/id #uuid "82a0784c-9e11-58e5-9dbf-fe2eada81cae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.404" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434079} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2e1dff-1f23-5716-942e-08efd9188695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.443" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fb991ce-4b7c-5de9-ab03-280608b86db1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.484" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434066} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b906fde-e2cb-5d14-8bd5-4b7e10aeffe7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "824977e3-712a-57cf-beb4-b57ff8f0ad52" :question/math-academy-id "q-108300" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X∼{χ}^{2}(k).$ Given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(4),$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X\\le x)=0.025.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $\\,0.072\\,$ | $\\,0.115\\,$ | $\\,0.216\\,$ | $\\,0.352\\,$ | $\\,0.584\\,$ |\n| $4$ | $\\,0.207\\,$ | $\\,0.297\\,$ | $\\,0.484\\,$ | $\\,0.711\\,$ | $\\,1.064\\,$ |\n| $5$ | $\\,0.412\\,$ | $\\,0.554\\,$ | $\\,0.831\\,$ | $\\,1.145\\,$ | $\\,1.610\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "The table shows probabilities in the form $P(X>x).$ So first, we express the desired probability in this form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>x) & =1-P(X\\le x) \\\\  & =1-0.025 \\\\  & =0.975\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼{χ}^{2}(4),$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=4.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $\\,0.207\\,$ | $\\,0.297\\,$ | $\\,0.484\\,$ | $\\,0.711\\,$ | $\\,1.064\\,$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X>0.484)=0.975.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=0.484.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec19c717-37bc-53c3-b09e-21a2fd2b77fa" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Chi-Square Probability Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acba8283-9370-5e8a-8629-c48cda23ad8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.825" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158649} {:answer/id #uuid "e78fb494-9343-52cc-bae6-91bf30fbfc15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.802" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158650} {:answer/id #uuid "ed92aa50-fed3-56f4-8351-dc2c198e8ade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.325" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158651} {:answer/id #uuid "15d3173d-dc89-5f8a-9277-00d8a3c7fdb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.352" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158652} {:answer/id #uuid "2cca45eb-a458-5398-b3ce-cc5f6cdec82f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.305" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed92aa50-fed3-56f4-8351-dc2c198e8ade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.325" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158651} :answer-field/id #uuid "42f7a27e-becb-5e70-b581-496783b99c55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e86557c-9410-5c5f-92dc-391547fa52a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-108307" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X∼{χ}^{2}(k).$ Given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(9),$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X\\le x)=0.05.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,0.989\\,$ | $\\,1.239\\,$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.167\\,$ | $\\,2.833\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.344\\,$ | $\\,1.646\\,$ | $\\,2.180\\,$ | $\\,2.733\\,$ | $\\,3.490\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,1.735\\,$ | $\\,2.088\\,$ | $\\,2.700\\,$ | $\\,3.325\\,$ | $\\,4.168\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "The table shows probabilities in the form $P(X>x).$ So first, we express the desired probability in this form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>x) & =1-P(X\\le x) \\\\  & =1-0.05 \\\\  & =0.95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼{χ}^{2}(9),$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=9.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $9$ | $\\,1.735\\,$ | $\\,2.088\\,$ | $\\,2.700\\,$ | $\\,3.325\\,$ | $\\,4.168\\,$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X>3.325)=0.950.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=3.325.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a75e6c3-16e3-50ab-bd36-a2c83ba9288a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability Involving Sums of Squared Standard Normal Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2fdfce5-219e-5618-a11d-545dab309523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34.382" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2fdfce5-219e-5618-a11d-545dab309523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34.382" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434083} :answer-field/id #uuid "649de251-3200-5093-ae42-9bf3c6fa21ab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "133afd92-ce15-5e18-bd34-c90446f47667" :question/math-academy-id "q-252035" :question/problem "Let the random variable $Y$ be defined as\n\n$$\nY={Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{25}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${Z}_{i}∼N(0,1)$ for $1\\le i\\le 25.$ Find the value $y$ such that $P(Y\\ge y)=0.1.$\n\n*Hint: The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X∼{χ}^{2}(k).$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $25$ | $\\,44.314\\,$ | $\\,37.652\\,$ | $\\,34.382\\,$ | $\\,32.282\\,$ | $\\,30.675\\,$ |\n| $26$ | $\\,45.642\\,$ | $\\,38.885\\,$ | $\\,35.563\\,$ | $\\,33.429\\,$ | $\\,31.795\\,$ |\n| $27$ | $\\,46.963\\,$ | $\\,40.113\\,$ | $\\,36.741\\,$ | $\\,34.574\\,$ | $\\,32.912\\,$ |\n\nThe value of $y$ that satisfies $P(Y\\ge y)=0.1$ is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "If ${Z}_{i}∼N(0,1)$ for $1\\le i\\le k$ are independent normal random variables, then the sum\n\n$$\n{Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{k}^{2}\n$$\n\nfollows a ${χ}^{2}(k)$ distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\nY={Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{25}^{2}∼{χ}^{2}(25).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the value $y$ such that $P(Y\\ge y)=0.1$ This can be found from the given table:\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $25$ | $\\,44.314\\,$ | $\\,37.652\\,$ | $\\,34.382\\,$ | $\\,32.282\\,$ | $\\,30.675\\,$ |\n| $26$ | $\\,45.642\\,$ | $\\,38.885\\,$ | $\\,35.563\\,$ | $\\,33.429\\,$ | $\\,31.795\\,$ |\n| $27$ | $\\,46.963\\,$ | $\\,40.113\\,$ | $\\,36.741\\,$ | $\\,34.574\\,$ | $\\,32.912\\,$ |\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 34.382)=0.1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=34.382.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a75e6c3-16e3-50ab-bd36-a2c83ba9288a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability Involving Sums of Squared Standard Normal Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "603c5297-e6ee-5028-b2ef-27ebcb3c175c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49.244" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "603c5297-e6ee-5028-b2ef-27ebcb3c175c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49.244" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434076} :answer-field/id #uuid "f463de34-0bb9-5c30-8c20-f9ac90effe8f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6afc0dfa-f42f-5b87-9c8b-8fbf362ef997" :question/math-academy-id "q-252038" :question/problem "Let the random variable $Y$ be defined as\n\n$$\nY={Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{40}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${Z}_{i}∼N(0,1)$ for $1\\le i\\le 40.$ Find the value $y$ such that $P(Y\\ge y)=0.15.$\n\n*Hint: The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X∼{χ}^{2}(k).$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $39$ | $\\,62.428\\,$ | $\\,54.572\\,$ | $\\,50.660\\,$ | $\\,48.126\\,$ | $\\,46.173\\,$ |\n| $40$ | $\\,63.691\\,$ | $\\,55.758\\,$ | $\\,51.805\\,$ | $\\,49.244\\,$ | $\\,47.269\\,$ |\n| $41$ | $\\,64.950\\,$ | $\\,56.942\\,$ | $\\,52.949\\,$ | $\\,50.360\\,$ | $\\,48.363\\,$ |\n\nThe value of $y$ that satisfies $P(Y\\ge y)=0.15$ is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "If ${Z}_{i}∼N(0,1)$ for $1\\le i\\le k$ are independent normal random variables, then the sum\n\n$$\n{Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{k}^{2}\n$$\n\nfollows a ${χ}^{2}(k)$ distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\nY={Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{40}^{2}∼{χ}^{2}(40).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the value $y$ such that $P(Y\\ge y)=0.15$ This can be found from the given table:\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $39$ | $\\,62.428\\,$ | $\\,54.572\\,$ | $\\,50.660\\,$ | $\\,48.126\\,$ | $\\,46.173\\,$ |\n| $40$ | $\\,63.691\\,$ | $\\,55.758\\,$ | $\\,51.805\\,$ | $\\,49.244\\,$ | $\\,47.269\\,$ |\n| $41$ | $\\,64.950\\,$ | $\\,56.942\\,$ | $\\,52.949\\,$ | $\\,50.360\\,$ | $\\,48.363\\,$ |\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 49.244)=0.15.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=49.244.$"}]}
